Once thought trading was a game for smart people, until witnessing several highly educated friends go bankrupt, while a few seasoned traders adhering to simple rules continued to profit for 15 years.

What truly determines the success or failure of trading is never the level of IQ, but the ability to counteract one's instincts.

1. The Trap of High IQ: Rational analysis often falls to emotional instincts

Cognitive Fallacy: A Wall Street survey shows that the dropout rate of Ivy League graduate traders in their first 3 years exceeds 50%, not due to insufficient analytical ability, but due to emotional fluctuations leading to violations;

Real Case: Mathematician Newton lamented after losing 10 years' worth of income during the South Sea Bubble: "I can calculate celestial trajectories, but I cannot predict human madness";

Essential Law: The market is in random fluctuation 90% of the time, and excessive reliance on analysis can lead to the "illusion of precision".

2. The Essence of Discipline: Using systems to combat human weaknesses

Rules above predictions
Successful traders' daily reviews do not focus on whether their market judgments are right or wrong, but whether they strictly adhere to stop-loss/profit-taking discipline;

Emotional Isolation Mechanism
Set a maximum daily loss limit (e.g., 5%), and upon reaching it, forcibly close the trading software to avoid retaliatory actions;

Mechanical Execution
Programmatic traders have a profit probability 2.3 times that of subjective traders, as systems eliminate the interference of "fear and greed".

3. Survivor Traits: The Transformation from "Smart" to "Stubborn"
Commonality 1: Average trading frequency ≤ 50 times per year, 80% of the time holding cash and waiting, refusing to be tempted by ineffective fluctuations;

Commonality 2: Establish a "trading checklist" (e.g., opening conditions, position ratios, stop-loss points), similar to a pilot checking a list before takeoff and landing;

Commonality 3: Forcibly transfer 30% of monthly profits out of the account to prevent overconfidence from leading to heavy gambling.

4. Cultivation Path: Discipline is an instinct honed through experience

Stage 1 (0-1 year): Focus on 1-2 technical indicators (e.g., MA60+RSI), repeatedly practice until muscle memory is formed;

Stage 2 (1-3 years): Record the emotional state for each trade (anxiety/greed/fear), regularly review the correlation between emotions and gains/losses;

Stage 3 (3+ years): Establish a personalized risk control system, such as "halt trading after 3 consecutive losses on the same day" and "withdraw principal after profits exceed 20%".

